 ## Background
-The concept of trapping particles for volumetric display was pioneered by Smalley et al. in 2018, who used a $405 \text{ nm}$ laser to trap $\approx 10 \mu m$ cellulose particles in air, illuminating them with coaxial RGB lasers to generate voxels [S1, unofficial]. This was the first method to adopt computer-hologram-based light irradiation patterns for volumetric displays, demonstrating that holographic design improves voxel count and brightness [S1, unofficial].
-
-Acoustic variants emerged earlier; in 2014, "Pixie Dust" proposed using 285 ultrasonic transducers across four speakers to create acoustic ponderomotive fields for levitating multiple particles [S1, unofficial]. In 2019, Hirayama et al. demonstrated particle trapping using standing acoustic waves [S1, unofficial]. The Kumagai method utilizes laser-excited bubbles in viscous liquids, offering long residence times for complex shape expression [S1, unofficial].
